-- title:
--   Degrees over κ((q)) are preserved by q ↦ qⁿ
-- statement:
--   Let $\kappa$ be a field and let $n$ be a nonzero natural number. Let $F$ and $F'$ be intermediate fields of the extension $\kappa \subseteq \kappa((q))$, where $\kappa((q))$ is `LaurentSeries κ`, the Hahn series over $\kappa$ with value group $\mathbb{Z}$. Write $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\kappa,n}$ for the ring endomorphism [`ModularCurve.qExpand κ n`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L25) of $\kappa((q))$ obtained by transporting supports along the additive monoid endomorphism of $\mathbb{Z}$ given by multiplication by $n$ (which is injective and order-preserving since $n > 0$); concretely it is the substitution $q \mapsto q^{n}$, sending $\sum_i a_i q^{i}$ to $\sum_i a_i q^{ni}$. Assume that the underlying subset of $\kappa((q))$ determined by $F'$ is exactly the image of the underlying subset determined by $F$ under $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\kappa,n}$. Then for every $x \in \kappa((q))$ the $F'$-rank of the intermediate field $F'(\mathrm{qExpand}_{\kappa,n}(x))$ obtained by adjoining $\mathrm{qExpand}_{\kappa,n}(x)$ to $F'$ inside $\kappa((q))$ equals the $F$-rank of $F(x)$. Ranks are `Module.finrank`, so both sides are $0$ when the respective extension is infinite-dimensional, that is when $x$ is transcendental over $F$.
--
--   This is the transport of relative degrees inside a Laurent series field along the substitution $q \mapsto q^{n}$: reading a subfield and an element in the variable $q^{n}$ changes neither the minimal polynomial's degree nor transcendence. It is used in the computation of the degree of the Igusa function field generated by $j(q^{N})$ over a modular function field, [`ModularCurve.finrank_adjoin_jqNModC_igusaFunctionFieldX1C_eq`](thm.html#ModularCurve.finrank_adjoin_jqNModC_igusaFunctionFieldX1C_eq).
